1. EPA and Magnolia Waco Properties, LLC, which does business as Magnolia Homes, have reached a settlement to resolve alleged violations of the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) Lead Renovation, Repair and Painting Rule (RRP Rule), related to home renovations conducted without adequate lead paint protections as depicted on the television program Fixer Upper.

2. Under the terms of the settlement, Magnolia will take steps to ensure compliance with lead-based paint regulations in future renovation projects, address lead-based paint hazards at high-risk homes in Waco, Texas, and educate the public to lead-based paint hazards and appropriate renovation procedures.

3. Magnolia will pay a civil penalty of $40,000.

4. In addition to the fine, Magnolia will pay to produce some lead-awareness bonus content for their web site and social-media accounts. The video will star Chip Gaines and aim to to make fans aware of how to be lead-safe, and hopefully it'll be ready in time for July 5.

5. The activities of Magnolia and similar renovation firms came to EPA's attention from their depiction on popular television shows. Magnolia's renovations were depicted on the HGTV series "Fixer Upper" and regularly viewed by well over 3 million households.

6. EPA's review of information it obtained with Magnolia's cooperation found evidence of noncompliance at renovations of 33 properties. Among the violations subsequently alleged by EPA were that Magnolia did not comply with the following requirements and precautions of the RRP Rule:

7. Obtain firm certification from EPA before performing renovations covered by the RRP Rule;

8. Assign a certified renovator to such renovations;

9. Provide home owners or occupants with an approved pamphlet about lead-based paint hazards prior to the renovation;

10. Post signs to clearly define the work area and warn people to remain outside that area;

11. Comply with the RRP Rule's work practice standards, such as closing doors and windows to the interior work areas
